{"step_index":1371,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-21T19:55:30Z","thinking":"**Investigating Transparency in WPF**\n\nI've got a breakthrough! Setting `AllowsTransparency=\"True\"` and `WindowStyle=\"None\"` in WPF seems to make `this.Opacity` work flawlessly without needing `SetLayeredWindowAttributes`! Now, I'm concerned about how `AllowsTransparency=\"True\"` might impact the WebView2 control. I need to dig deeper.\n\n\n**Confirming WebView2 Compatibility**\n\nI've confirmed WebView2's seamless compatibility with `AllowsTransparency=\"True\"`, provided `DefaultBackgroundColor` is set. Setting `WindowStyle=\"None\"` and `AllowsTransparency=\"True\"` in the XAML will allow for direct manipulation of the `Opacity` property. This approach bypasses the complex Win32 calls. Setting `WebView2.DefaultBackgroundColor = System.Drawing.Color.Transparent;` is the key. It looks like the WebView2 control is ready to be handled out of the box with the new settings!\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":false,"Description":"Enable WPF native transparency for flawless WebView2 alpha blending.","EndLine":11,"Instruction":"Add AllowsTransparency and Background=Transparent","ReplacementContent":"        Title=\"AMD Audio Device Host\" Height=\"800\" Width=\"1200\"\n        WindowStyle=\"None\"\n        AllowsTransparency=\"True\"\n        Background=\"Transparent\"\n        Topmost=\"True\"\n        ShowInTaskbar=\"False\"\n        WindowStartupLocation=\"CenterScreen\">\n    <Grid Background=\"Transparent\">","StartLine":5,"TargetContent":"        Title=\"AMD Audio Device Host\" Height=\"800\" Width=\"1200\"\n        WindowStyle=\"None\"\n        Background=\"#090A0E\"\n        Topmost=\"True\"\n        ShowInTaskbar=\"False\"\n        WindowStartupLocation=\"CenterScreen\">\n    <Grid Background=\"#090A0E\">","TargetFile":"c:\\Users\\Arthur Henrique\\Documents\\GitHub\\Spectra\\src-csharp\\UI\\M