{"step_index":1385,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-21T19:56:56Z","thinking":"**Investigating Ghost Mode**\n\nI've been looking into `SetGhostMode`. I've determined that Ghost Mode needs `WS_EX_TRANSPARENT`. I was unsure if `WS_EX_TRANSPARENT` works with WPF's `AllowsTransparency=\"True\"`, but I've confirmed that it does! `WS_EX_TRANSPARENT` enables hit-testing pass-through flawlessly for WPF layered windows.\n\n\n**Refining Ghost Mode Implementation**\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI'm now focusing on the implementation details for enabling and disabling Ghost Mode. My current thought is to ensure the transparency style is set appropriately, and then toggle `WS_EX_TRANSPARENT` via `SetWindowLongPtr`. I also plan to call `SetWindowPos` to flush the hit-test attributes after the change. I'm also ensuring that transparency presets work correctly.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"& \"$env:LocalAppData\\Microsoft\\dotnet\\dotnet.exe\" publish src-csharp\\Spectra.csproj -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained -p:PublishSingleFile=true -o publish\nCopy-Item -Path \"publish\\AmdAudioDevice.exe\" -Destination \"AmdAudioDevice.exe\" -Force","Cwd":"c:\\Users\\Arthur Henrique\\Documents\\GitHub\\Spectra","WaitMsBeforeAsync":20000,"toolAction":"Rebuilding AmdAudioDevice.exe with WPF native transparency","toolSummary":"Rebuild with WPF transparency"}}]}
