{"step_index":1399,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-21T20:10:04Z","content":"Peraí — você disse que abriu **\"Spectre.exe\"**. O executável novo se chama **`AmdAudioDevice.exe`**! Você pode estar rodando o binário antigo que não tem nenhuma dessas correções.\n\nMas vou além disso — vou primeiro checar o log de debug pra ver se o hook sequer está sendo instalado, e vou reescrever o `HotkeyManager` de forma à prova de falhas.","thinking":"The user is frustrated because the hotkeys still aren't working. They mentioned they opened \"Spectre.exe\" - but the executable was renamed to \"AmdAudioDevice.exe\". Let me check if they're running the old binary or the new one.\n\nBut more importantly, let me think about what's actually going wrong. The user says hotkeys aren't working. Let me think about this systematically:\n\n1. The low-level keyboard hook (`WH_KEYBOARD_LL`) requires a message pump running on the thread that installed the hook. In WPF, the main UI thread has a message pump (Dispatcher), but the hook needs to be installed on that thread.\n2. Tracing the code flow, the hotkey manager gets initialized with the window handle on load, then registers global hotkeys, installing the hook with the current module handle. I suspect the issue is that `GetModuleHandle` might return null in a single-file published app since the module name resolution can behave unexpectedly in that scenario.\n\n3. There's also a timing constraint: WH_KEYBOARD_LL hooks must process within roughly 300ms or Windows silently removes them, so if Dispatcher.Invoke blocks waiting on the UI thread, that could be killing the hook. I want to check the debug log to confirm hooks are actually installing, and I suspect the real culprit is that for single-file apps, the module handle lookup fails and the IntPtr.Zero fallback doesn't work either -- so I should try using LoadLibrary on user32.dll as the module handle instead, a known workaround for this exact problem.\n\nI also notice the user mentioned running \"Spectre.exe\" rather than the renamed binary, so they might be testing an old build without any of these fixes applied. Either way, I'll add thorough logging around the hook setup so we can actually see what's failing at runtime.","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"if (Test-Path \"spectra_debug.log\") { Get-Content \"spectra_debug.log\" -Tail 50 } else { Write-Output \"Log file not found\" }","Cwd":"c:\\Users\\Arthur Henrique\\Documents\\GitHub\\Spectra","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking debug log","toolSummary":"Check debug log"}}]}
